From e0212741327166e49049d6deed6877688df7bd57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Delgado <admin@sytue.eu>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 03:29:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(mobile/ios): app refresh tasks and launches without
 a scene

iOS can launch an app with no scene, for an app refresh task (BGAppRefreshTask) or a
background notification, and the whole main function runs then too. The mobile driver
now implements the optional mobile.BackgroundDriver:

- LaunchedInBackground: UIKit launched the process for background work and no scene has
  connected yet.
- OnFirstScene: run what needs a user interface when the first scene connects, on the
  main thread and before that scene's URLs; at once elsewhere. A scene UIKit connects
  outside the foreground waits, with its URLs, until it first enters the foreground.
- ProtectedDataAvailable: false while the device is locked, followed through the
  application delegate.
- OnBackgroundRefresh: the handler of the task named by the first entry of
  BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers. It is registered before launching finishes,
  requested again each time the app moves to the background and when the handler
  returns, and completed exactly once: an expiring task cancels the handler's context
  and fails as soon as the handler returns, or after a 500 ms grace; one that expires
  before its handler starts (the main thread busy) fails at once and never runs it.
  iOS suspends the app when the task completes, and kills one suspended holding a file
  lock in a shared container (0xdead10cc): the grace lets the handler let go of it.
  Each task asks UIKit for isProtectedDataAvailable on the main thread before the
  handler runs, as the delegate notifications can reach a suspended app late.

Apps without those Info.plist keys behave as before. The portable state machine is
tested on every platform; the Objective-C bridge is pinned by source contracts, as it
cannot be compiled outside Xcode.

darwin_ios.m is compiled by cgo without ARC: a scene whose connection waits for its first
foreground keeps the connection options in a property, released only after the
connection used them (releasing them first crashed every launch, as a regular launch
connects its scene unattached), and sceneDidBecomeActive connects it too should UIKit
skip sceneWillEnterForeground. The source contracts read the native sources with LF line
endings and fail, instead of panicking, when a marker is missing.

